---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="fuel-economy-converter">
  <UnitConverter client:load dimension="fuel" from="mpg-us" to="l100km" value="30" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert fuel economy</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Type your figure into either field. The tool handles the reciprocal relation, so you do not need to remember the direction of the division.</li>
      <li>Pick the units, and read the group labels: the first group counts fuel per distance (lower is better) and the second counts distance per fuel (higher is better).</li>
      <li>Check the formula panel. It shows a division, not a multiplication, which is why this conversion causes errors.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Why this conversion is not a multiplication</h2>
    <p>
      Miles per gallon and litres per 100 km measure the same thing in opposite directions. One is distance
      divided by fuel; the other is fuel divided by distance. The conversion takes a reciprocal:
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>L/100 km = 235.214583 ÷ mpg (US)</strong>, and <strong>mpg (US) = 235.214583 ÷
      L/100 km</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      For imperial gallons the constant is 282.480936. Both constants come from exact definitions:
      3.785411784 L per US gallon and 1.609344 km per mile, so 100 × 3.785411784 ÷ 1.609344 = 235.214583.
    </p>
    <p>
      As a result, improvements are not linear. From 15 to 20 mpg saves 1.67 gallons per 100 miles; from 40 to
      45 mpg saves only 0.28 gallons over the same distance. Replacing the least efficient vehicle in a fleet
      saves much more fuel than a small improvement to an efficient one. The L/100 km scale shows this
      clearly; mpg hides it.
    </p>

    <h2>US mpg and UK mpg are different numbers</h2>
    <p>
      The imperial gallon is 4.54609 litres; the US gallon is 3.785411784. The UK gallon is 20 % larger, so the
      same car gets about 20 % more miles per gallon in UK figures. A car advertised at 40 mpg in Britain is a
      33 mpg car in America. Both figures are correct; they use different gallons.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>L/100 km</th><th>mpg (US)</th><th>mpg (UK)</th><th>km/L</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>4</td><td>58.8</td><td>70.6</td><td>25.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5</td><td>47.0</td><td>56.5</td><td>20.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6</td><td>39.2</td><td>47.1</td><td>16.7</td></tr>
          <tr><td>7</td><td>33.6</td><td>40.4</td><td>14.3</td></tr>
          <tr><td>8</td><td>29.4</td><td>35.3</td><td>12.5</td></tr>
          <tr><td>10</td><td>23.5</td><td>28.2</td><td>10.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>12</td><td>19.6</td><td>23.5</td><td>8.3</td></tr>
          <tr><td>15</td><td>15.7</td><td>18.8</td><td>6.7</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>What counts as good</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Under 5 L/100 km (over 47 US mpg): excellent; small diesels and full hybrids.</li>
      <li>5 to 8 L/100 km: typical for a modern petrol family car driven sensibly.</li>
      <li>8 to 12 L/100 km: larger petrol cars, SUVs, or mostly short urban trips.</li>
      <li>Over 12 L/100 km (under 20 US mpg): high consumption; big engines, towing, or heavy city driving.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Manufacturer figures come from standardised test cycles (WLTP in Europe, EPA in the US), and real-world
      consumption is usually 10 to 25 % higher. To know your own number, fill the tank, reset the trip meter,
      and divide at the next fill.
    </p>

    <h2>Electric cars and the kWh option</h2>
    <p>
      Electric vehicles are rated in kWh per 100 km, or in miles per kWh in the US. This fuel economy converter
      has a kWh/100 km unit that maps onto the fuel scale with the EPA equivalence of about 33.7 kWh per US
      gallon of petrol, about 8.9 kWh per litre. You can then put an EV and a petrol car on the same axis for
      an energy comparison.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use it only as an energy comparison, not a fuel comparison. An EV that uses 18 kWh/100 km is not "doing
      2 L/100 km" in a physical sense. It uses the energy of two litres of petrol, and an internal combustion
      engine loses most of that energy as heat. For running costs, compare the price of a kWh with the price
      of a litre directly.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
